Q1. Deadlock Debugging 
Problem Identification:
  The deadlock occurs because Process P1 locks R1 and waits for R2, while Process P2 locks R2 and waits for R1. This creates a circular wait, where each process is waiting for a resource held by the other. All four Coffman conditions (Mutual Exclusion, Hold and Wait, No Preemption, and Circular Wait) are satisfied, resulting in a deadlock.
Debugging:
Tracing the execution:
  * P1 acquires R1.
  * P2 acquires R2.
  * P1 requests R2 but it is unavailable.
  * P2 requests R1 but it is unavailable.
  * Both processes remain blocked indefinitely.
Optimization:
  Enforce a fixed resource allocation order by requiring all processes to acquire R1 before R2. This eliminates the circular wait condition without changing the process logic.
Analysis:
  The proposed solution guarantees that resources are requested in a consistent order, preventing deadlocks while maintaining correct program execution.

Q2. CPU Utilization Problem 
Problem Identification:
  The repeated transitions Ready → Waiting → Ready indicate that the processes are I/O-bound. They frequently leave the CPU to wait for I/O operations, causing the CPU to remain idle even when processes are available.
Debugging:
  The scheduler repeatedly switches processes because they spend more time waiting for I/O than executing CPU instructions. This leads to only 30% CPU utilization.
Optimization:
  * Increase the degree of multiprogramming so another process can execute while one waits for I/O.
  * Improve I/O scheduling or use asynchronous I/O to reduce waiting time.
Analysis:
  These optimizations keep the CPU busy by overlapping computation with I/O operations, thereby increasing CPU utilization and improving system throughput.

Q3. Memory Bottleneck Debugging
Problem Identification:
  The application has 25 threads, each using 1 MB stack, along with a 150 MB heap. Frequent page faults indicate inefficient memory usage and poor memory locality, reducing performance.
Debugging:
  Each page fault forces the operating system to fetch data from secondary storage into RAM. Frequent page faults significantly increase memory access time and slow the multi-threaded application.
Optimization:
  * Reduce the number of active threads or decrease the stack size of each thread.
  * Optimize heap allocation and improve memory locality to reduce page faults.
Analysis:
  Reducing unnecessary memory consumption minimizes page faults, improves cache efficiency, and increases overall application performance.

Q4. Starvation Debugging
Problem Identification:
  Process P5 is placed in Q3 (Batch Queue), while higher-priority queues (Q1 and Q2) continuously receive new processes. As a result, P5 never receives CPU time, causing starvation.
Debugging:
  The scheduler always selects higher-priority processes first, preventing lower-priority processes from executing.
Optimization:
  * Implement Aging to gradually increase the priority of waiting processes.
  * Allocate periodic CPU time to lower-priority queues to ensure fairness.
Analysis:
  Aging guarantees that every process eventually receives CPU time, preventing starvation while preserving the benefits of priority scheduling.

Q5. Context-Switch Overhead Debugging
Problem Identification:
  The server performs 20,000 context switches per second because the time quantum is only 1 ms, thread burst times average 2 ms, and I/O requests occur very frequently. This causes excessive CPU time to be spent on context switching rather than executing processes.
Debugging:
  The scheduler preempts threads too frequently, forcing the operating system to repeatedly save and restore process states. Frequent I/O operations further increase the number of context switches.
Optimization:
  * Increase the time quantum to reduce unnecessary preemptions.
  * Reduce I/O waiting by using buffering or asynchronous I/O, thereby decreasing context-switch frequency.
Analysis:
  These optimizations reduce scheduling overhead, improve CPU efficiency, and increase overall system performance by allowing processes to execute for longer periods before being interrupted.
